.Dd August 27, 2026
.Dt SOWA-PARTITIONER 8
.Os Sowa
.Sh NAME
.Nm sowa-partitioner
.Nd look at the disks, lay out partitions, build RAID and LVM
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -yes | Fl -dry-run | Fl -no-format
.Op Fl -allow-loop | Fl -sector-size Ns = Ns Ar n
.Nm
.Cm list
.Nm
.Cm show Ar disk
.Nm
.Cm firmware
.Nm
.Cm raw Ar file
.Nm
.Cm label Ar disk Cm gpt | dos
.Nm
.Cm scheme Ar disk Cm gpt | dos
.Op Fl -esp-size Ns = Ns Ar size
.Nm
.Cm create Ar disk Ar kind Ar size
.Nm
.Cm delete Ar disk Ar number
.Nm
.Cm wipe Ar disk
.Op Fl -mode Ns = Ns Ar mode
.Op Fl -size Ns = Ns Ar size
.Op Fl -partition Ns = Ns Ar number
.Nm
.Cm raid Fl -level Ns = Ns Ar n Fl -name Ns = Ns Ar node Ar partition ...
.Nm
.Cm lvm Fl -vg Ns = Ns Ar name Ar device ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the program that gets a disk into a state something can be installed onto.
.Xr sowa-setup 8
does its own partitioning of one whole disk in one fixed shape;
.Nm
does the general case: several disks, an existing table to read and keep,
partitions placed by hand, and md or LVM stacked on top afterwards.
.Pp
With no subcommand it runs as a full-screen program.
The first screen lists everything on the machine that can hold a partition
table; opening one of those shows a proportional map of what is on it beside a
table of its partitions and free space.
With a subcommand it does one thing and exits, so the same tool serves an
install being done by hand and a script preparing a machine.
.Pp
The two screens divide the work the way the disk does.
Anything that replaces a whole disk - writing a new table, applying one of the
predefined layouts - is on the first screen, next to the disk it would replace.
Anything that edits one partition is on the second.
.Ss The map
Every partition and every gap gets a share of the width in proportion to its
size, except that a partition never gets none: a 1 MiB BIOS Boot Partition on a
2 TB disk is a hundred-thousandth of the disk and still has to be visible and
selectable.
A partition that is on the disk is drawn solid, one that is only in the plan is
drawn hatched, and free space is drawn light.
.Pp
The selected segment stays whole and turns blinking bright white, with a thick
rule under it in its own colour.
White is the largest difference available without leaving the palette, blink is
the one attribute that cannot be mistaken for part of the picture, and the rule
keeps the type colour so the association survives on a terminal that renders
neither.
.Ss What can be partitioned
Disks, the md arrays built out of their partitions, and the logical volumes
built out of those.
All three are block devices with room for a partition table in them, so all
three appear on the first screen and all three open into the same editor: an
array can be partitioned, and so can a volume.
.Pp
A raw image - a regular file being treated as a disk - appears there too when
one was named.
See
.Sx RAW IMAGES .
.Ss Partition numbers
The number a partition has is the one the table gives it, and it is left alone.
This matters more than it sounds: a cloud image's root is commonly partition 1
while its BIOS Boot Partition is 14 and its ESP is 15, so numbering by position
on the disk would call them 3, 1 and 2 - wrong on the screen, and wrong in a
command that names one.
Applying a plan writes each partition into the slot it already had, and a new
partition takes the lowest number nothing is using.
.Ss The plan
Nothing is written to a disk until it is applied.
Everything up to that point edits an in-memory plan - a list of partitions with
a start, a length and a kind - which is drawn beside the table that is really
on the disk.
Applying turns that plan into one
.Xr sfdisk 8
script and one call, and the pending changes are listed and confirmed before it
runs.
.Pp
Writing a new table and applying a predefined layout are the exceptions: both
replace the whole disk rather than editing part of it, both are on the first
screen, and both happen when they are confirmed.
The layout shows what is on the disk now, what will be there instead, and a map
of the result, before it asks.
.Pp
Rewriting a table renumbers what is on it, and on GPT it would ordinarily hand
every partition a fresh UUID.
Both would break an
.Xr fstab 5
that names partitions by PARTUUID, for partitions whose contents were never
touched.
So the identity of everything that was already there is carried through the
rewrite: the GPT partition UUIDs are read out of the old table and written back
into the new one, and an MBR disk's four-byte signature - the first half of
every PARTUUID on it - is preserved the same way.
Only genuinely new partitions get new identities.
.Pp
Every start and length is a whole number of mebibytes.
GPT reserves the last one for its backup header, and MBR leaves the first one
free as the gap GRUB's i386-pc core is embedded into.
.Ss Partition kinds
.Bl -tag -width "biosboot" -compact
.It Cm ext4
An ext4 filesystem, made when the plan is applied.
.It Cm xfs
An XFS filesystem.
.It Cm btrfs
A Btrfs filesystem.
.It Cm efi
A FAT32 EFI System Partition.
.It Cm biosboot
The 1 MiB BIOS Boot Partition GRUB embeds into on GPT, left empty.
MBR has no such type and does not need one.
.It Cm raid
A Linux RAID member, left empty for
.Xr mdadm 8 .
.It Cm lvm
An LVM2 physical volume, left empty for
.Xr pvcreate 8 .
.El
.Pp
The ext4 filesystems it makes have
.Cm orphan_file
and
.Cm metadata_csum_seed
switched off, for the same reason
.Xr sowa-setup 8
does it: e2fsprogs 1.47 turns both on by default and GRUB 2.12's ext2 driver
can read neither, so a
.Pa /boot
made without this lands at a rescue prompt instead of a boot menu.
.Pp
A partition read off a disk that is none of these keeps whatever type code it
was found with and is shown under that code's usual name.
Such a partition can be kept, moved, resized or deleted; changing its type is
what makes it one of the kinds above, and doing that marks it to be
reformatted.
.Ss Sizes
Sizes are read the way
.Xr sfdisk 8
reads them, because
.Xr sfdisk 8
is what eventually receives them: a bare unit letter is a power of 1024, a
two-letter
.Dq B
spelling is a power of 1000, and the explicit
.Dq iB
spellings are powers of 1024.
So
.Ar 10G
and
.Ar 10GiB
are the same number and
.Ar 10GB
is a smaller one.
.Ar 2048s
is a number of sectors of the disk's own logical sector size.
.Pp
A percentage is of whatever is being filled - the free region a new partition
is being placed in, the disk a wipe is being sized against - so that
.Ar 100%
always means
.Dq the rest of it .
.Pp
A number with no unit at all is refused.
It is the one input whose intent cannot be read off the text, and the answer
bytes would give is a partition of the minimum size created without complaint.
.Ss Wiping
Wiping is a separate operation from partitioning and happens immediately;
there is nothing to apply afterwards.
Its default is the narrow one: the regions that hold a partition table and the
first and last mebibyte of every partition that is on the disk now.
That is where filesystem superblocks, LVM labels and every version of an mdadm
superblock live, so it is what makes a disk stop being recognised as whatever
it used to be - without writing over a terabyte to do it.
.Pp
The whole disk, a stated size or percentage from the start of it, or one whole
partition can be wiped instead.
Overlapping regions are merged before anything is written, and a wipe long
enough to be worth watching reports its progress.
.Ss Firmware
Which partition table to use is decided by how the machine's firmware finds a
bootloader, so
.Nm
works that out rather than asking.
.Pa /sys/firmware/efi
exists if and only if the kernel was entered through EFI boot services, which
is the question that matters: a UEFI machine booted through its compatibility
support module is a BIOS machine as far as the disk is concerned, and will
still be one on the next boot.
Secure Boot is read from the
.Va SecureBoot
EFI variable through efivarfs.
.Pp
GPT is the recommendation either way.
On a machine that booted through UEFI it is effectively required, because UEFI
firmware is not obliged to look for an EFI System Partition on an MBR disk and
plenty of it does not.
On a machine that booted through BIOS the GPT layout below carries a BIOS Boot
Partition as well as an ESP, so the disk boots under either firmware.
.Pp
Secure Boot is reported because of what it does to the result rather than to
the layout: Sowa's GRUB image is not signed and no Sowa key is in anybody's
firmware, so a machine with Secure Boot enabled refuses to load it however the
disk is laid out.
No partition layout works around that; it has to be turned off in the firmware
setup.
.Ss Volume groups
.Cm V
on either screen manages LVM.
With no volume group on the machine it goes straight to building one out of
partitions; with one or more it offers those as well.
Inside a group it lists the volumes, how big each is, what filesystem is on it
and where it is mounted, and the free space left - and creates, removes and
formats volumes with the same size widget partitions use.
.Pp
Volumes are made when they are asked for rather than planned first.
LVM has its own idea of a transaction and there is nothing to be gained by
inventing a second one on top of it.
.Ss Predefined layouts
Two layouts that are known to boot Sowa, so that the common case is one
keystroke rather than four sizes typed by hand.
Both put the whole of the rest of the disk into an ext4 root, which is what
.Xr sowa-setup 8
would have made.
.Bl -tag -width "dos" -compact
.It Cm gpt
1 MiB BIOS boot, 512 MiB EFI System, ext4 for the rest.
.It Cm dos
512 MiB EFI System, ext4 for the rest.
.El
.Pp
The MBR layout has no BIOS Boot Partition because MBR has no such type: GRUB's
i386-pc core goes in the gap between the MBR and the first partition, which the
alignment leaves free anyway.
The ESP is in both because it costs half a gigabyte and lets the same disk boot
under either firmware.
.Sh SUBCOMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm list
Every disk, with its partition table, size and partitions.
Needs no privilege.
.It Cm show Ar disk
One disk in detail: the table, the sector size, and every partition and free
region with its start, length and contents.
Needs no privilege.
.It Cm firmware
How this machine booted, whether Secure Boot is enabled, which layout suits it
and why.
Needs no privilege.
.It Cm raw Ar file
Open the full-screen program on a raw image.
Needs no privilege beyond write access to the file itself.
.It Cm label Ar disk Cm gpt | dos
Write an empty partition table, discarding whatever the current one describes.
.It Cm scheme Ar disk Cm gpt | dos
Lay the disk out with one of the predefined layouts above.
.Fl -esp-size
sets the EFI System Partition size, which defaults to 512MiB and may not be
under 34 MiB - below that a FAT32 filesystem cannot reach the 65525 clusters
.Xr mkfs.fat 8
requires.
.It Cm create Ar disk Ar kind Ar size
Add one partition of that kind, in the largest free region on the disk.
.Ar size
may be a percentage of that region.
.It Cm delete Ar disk Ar number
Remove one partition from the table.
What follows it is renumbered.
.It Cm wipe Ar disk
Write zeros over it, in the mode
.Fl -mode
names:
.Cm signatures
(the default),
.Cm full ,
.Cm size
with
.Fl -size ,
or
.Cm partition
with
.Fl -partition .
.It Cm raid Fl -level Ns = Ns Ar n Fl -name Ns = Ns Ar node Ar partition ...
Create a RAID array from partitions that exist.
Levels 0, 1, 5, 6 and 10 are offered, with 1.2 metadata - mdadm's own default,
and the only version the kernel assembles a root from without help.
.Fl -record
appends the resulting array to
.Pa /etc/mdadm.conf ,
which is what
.Pa rc.sysinit
consults when it assembles arrays before mounting
.Xr fstab 5 .
.It Cm lvm Fl -vg Ns = Ns Ar name Ar device ...
Create physical volumes on the named devices and a volume group over them.
With
.Fl -lv
and
.Fl -size
it then creates one logical volume, and with
.Fl -fs
it makes a filesystem on it.
A
.Fl -size
given as a percentage is passed to
.Xr lvcreate 8
as a share of the free extents, which is more accurate than anything computed
outside LVM.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -yes , Fl y
Do not ask before writing.
.It Fl -dry-run , Fl n
Print every command that would be run and write nothing.
Every destructive step in this program goes through one place, so this covers
all of them, and it does not require privilege.
.It Fl -no-format
Write the partition table but make no filesystems.
.It Fl -allow-loop
Treat loop devices as disks.
They are not disks and are left out of the listing, which is what stops a live
system's own medium from appearing as a target; this is for testing.
.It Fl -sector-size Ns = Ns Ar n
The sector size a raw image is treated as having: 512, 1024, 2048 or 4096.
A file has no opinion about this the way a disk does, and 512 is what every
tool assumes of one unless it is told otherwise.
Ignored for real devices, which are asked.
.El
.Sh RAW IMAGES
A raw image is a regular file partitioned as though it were a disk.
Every subcommand takes one wherever it takes a disk, and
.Cm raw
opens the full-screen program on it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fallocate -l 1G fake_disk
sowa-partitioner raw fake_disk
.Ed
.Pp
Everything a partition table needs is in the bytes of the file and none of it
is in the fact that a disk is a disk, so the table, the free-space arithmetic,
the layouts and the wipe all work unchanged.
Writing to a file needs no privilege beyond the file's own permissions, so this
does not ask for root.
.Pp
The one thing a file cannot have is partition device nodes, and
.Xr mkfs 8
has to be pointed at one.
So filesystems inside an image are made by attaching it with
.Dq losetup -P ,
which gives it nodes for as long as it stays attached; that needs root.
Without it the partition table is still written and the filesystems are simply
not made, which is said rather than skipped quietly - attach the image and run
.Xr mkfs 8
afterwards.
.Pp
An image must be a whole number of sectors and at least two mebibytes.
.Sh KEYS
In the full-screen program, on the disk list:
.Bl -tag -width "Enter" -compact
.It Enter
Open the disk under the cursor.
.It Ic s
Lay the whole thing out for Sowa, with a preview of the result.
.It Ic l
Write a new, empty partition table.
.It Ic f
What the firmware means for the layout.
.It Ic R
Build a RAID array out of partitions.
.It Ic V
Manage volume groups and the volumes in them.
.It Ic r
Look again.
.It Ic q
Quit.
.El
.Pp
On a disk:
.Bl -tag -width "Enter" -compact
.It Ic n
A new partition in the free region under the cursor.
.It Ic d
Delete the partition under the cursor.
.It Ic t
Change its kind, which marks it to be reformatted.
.It Ic z
Resize it.
Only the end moves; no filesystem is resized with it.
.It Ic f
What the firmware means for the layout.
.It Ic w
Wipe with zeros.
.It Ic a
Apply the plan.
.It Ic u
Forget the plan and read the disk again.
.It Ic q
Go back.
.El
.Pp
In a volume group,
.Ic n
makes a volume,
.Ic d
removes one and
.Ic t
puts a filesystem on one.
.Pp
Where a size is asked for, the left and right arrows move a slider and the
shifted arrows or PgUp and PgDn move it further; Home and End go to the
smallest and largest it may be.
Typing a size replaces it, and the slider follows what is typed as it is typed.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev SOWA_PARTITIONER_ALLOW_LOOP
Set to 1, the same as
.Fl -allow-loop .
.It Ev NO_COLOR
Set to anything, colour is not used.
.It Ev LANG , Ev LC_CTYPE , Ev LC_ALL
A UTF-8 locale draws the partition map with block characters, and anything else
draws it with ASCII.
The Linux console draws box characters only when it has been put in UTF-8 mode,
and a serial console draws whatever is on the other end of the line.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/mdadm.conf
Where
.Cm raid Fl -record
records an array so it is assembled under the same name at every boot.
.It Pa /sys/firmware/efi
Present if and only if this system booted through UEFI.
.It Pa /sys/firmware/efi/efivars
Where the Secure Boot state is read from, when efivarfs is mounted there.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Look before touching anything:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sowa-partitioner list
sowa-partitioner show /dev/sda
sowa-partitioner firmware
.Ed
.Pp
Try a layout out on a file first, where nothing is at stake:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
fallocate -l 10G fake_disk
sowa-partitioner scheme fake_disk gpt
sowa-partitioner show fake_disk
.Ed
.Pp
Lay a disk out for Sowa, having been told which table suits the machine:
.Pp
.Dl sowa-partitioner scheme /dev/sda gpt
.Pp
See what that would do without doing any of it:
.Pp
.Dl sowa-partitioner --dry-run scheme /dev/sda gpt
.Pp
A disk that came from somewhere else, cleared of every signature it arrived
with and laid out again, unattended:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sowa-partitioner --yes wipe /dev/sdb
sowa-partitioner --yes scheme /dev/sdb gpt --esp-size=1G
.Ed
.Pp
A mirror of two disks, with a volume group on it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sowa-partitioner --yes label /dev/sdb gpt
sowa-partitioner --yes label /dev/sdc gpt
sowa-partitioner --yes create /dev/sdb raid 100%
sowa-partitioner --yes create /dev/sdc raid 100%
sowa-partitioner --yes raid --level=1 --name=/dev/md0 --record \e
    /dev/sdb1 /dev/sdc1
sowa-partitioner --yes lvm --vg=data --lv=files --size=100% --fs=xfs /dev/md0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sowa-setup 8 ,
.Xr sowa-bootstrap 8 ,
.Xr sowa-chroot 8 ,
.Xr sfdisk 8 ,
.Xr wipefs 8 ,
.Xr mdadm 8 ,
.Xr lvm 8
.Sh CAVEATS
An MBR table here holds four primary partitions and nothing else.
Extended and logical partitions exist to get past that limit on disks that
predate GPT, and the answer on any disk that can hold a modern system is to use
GPT instead.
.Pp
Resizing moves the end of a partition and does not move or resize the
filesystem inside it.
Growing one is harmless and the filesystem can be grown into the new space
afterwards with
.Xr resize2fs 8 ,
.Xr xfs_growfs 8
or
.Xr btrfs 8 ;
shrinking one below the size of its filesystem destroys it.
Nothing here shrinks a filesystem first.
.Pp
Applying a plan rewrites the whole table, but it does not renumber it: each
partition is written back into the slot it already had, and the PARTUUIDs and
the disk's own identity are carried across with it.
Deleting a partition frees its number for the next one created, and does not
move any other.
.Pp
The kernel will not re-read a partition table while anything on the disk is
open, so a disk with a mounted partition, an assembled array member or an
active physical volume on it cannot be repartitioned.
.Nm
checks for mounts and refuses rather than writing a table the kernel then
declines to read.
.Pp
Building a RAID array or a volume group takes device nodes, not intentions, so
the plan has to have been applied before either can be built on its partitions.
.Pp
Nothing here resizes or moves a filesystem, mounts anything, or writes an
.Xr fstab 5 .
Making a disk bootable is
.Xr sowa-setup 8
and
.Xr sowa-bootstrap 8 ;
this program stops at the point where those can be pointed at the result.
